{
  "abstract": "Erdős Problem 707 asks whether every finite Sidon set of natural numbers is contained in a perfect difference set. Every Sidon set of size 3 extends (Sawin). For size 4, Mueller (MathOverflow answer 502053) found {0,1,3,11} and {0,1,4,11} and showed that neither lies in any Singer (Desarguesian) difference set, but whether they extend to a non-Desarguesian cyclic plane is open. This formalization proves, in Lean 4 with Mathlib and only the standard axioms, two restrictions that need no assumption on the plane: (1) neither set lies in a perfect difference set of even order (any modulus n), by Baer's argument that 2 is then a multiplier; (2) {0,1,3,11} lies in no finite perfect difference set whose order is divisible by 2 or by 3, by Hall's multiplier theorem, which is proved here from scratch for planar difference sets (Frobenius in the group algebra (ZMod p)[ZMod n]) with no prime-power assumption. The question for orders with all prime factors at least 5 (for {0,1,3,11}) and for odd orders (for {0,1,4,11}) is not settled.",
